f may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 2 to 4 inches of rain has fallen across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T23:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.f94cd009096e4e9c7294adf8ca346afee949e6bc.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.f94cd009096e4e9c7294adf8ca346afee949e6bc.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 4:08PM MST until November 2 at 4:15PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"Significant and historic flooding is occurring across southwest\nColorado and southeast Utah. Widespread rainfall will continue\nthrough Monday. Excessive runoff will also increase flooding risks.\nMany local roads have become flooded and impassable. Some roads have\nwashed out, or collapsed completely. If you live in the warned area,\nit is recommended to not travel this evening or overnight. This is a\nPARTICULARLY DANGEROUS SITUATION.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding caused by excessive rainfall will continue.\nThis is a PARTICULARLY DANGEROUS SITUATION.\n\n* WHERE...Western Archuleta County in southwestern Colorado...\nWestern Dolores County in southwestern Colorado...\nSoutheastern La Plata County in southwestern Colorado...\nMontezuma County in southwestern Colorado...\nWest Central San Miguel County in southwestern Colorado...\nSouthern San Juan County in southeastern Utah...\n\n* WHEN...Until 415 PM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is occurring. Numerous roads remain\nclosed due to flooding.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 359 PM MST, local law enforcement reported heavy rain in the\nwarned area. Flooding is already occurring. Between 1.5 and 3\ninches of rain have fallen.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nDurango, Blanding, Mexican Hat, Cortez, Pagosa Springs, Dolores,\nDove Creek, Ignacio, Aneth, Montezuma Creek, White Mesa, Bluff,\nTselakai Dezza, Halchita, Goulding, Hovenweep National Monument,\nTowaoc, Arriola, Yellow Jacket and Lewis.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T16:15:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO; Dolores, CO; La Plata, CO; Montezuma, CO; San Miguel, CO; San Juan, UT","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.616c4025c42723c2fe6d8b10282486a4525dd113.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.616c4025c42723c2fe6d8b10282486a4525dd113.001.1"},{"event":"Avalanche Advisory","headline":"Avalanche Advisory issued October 31 at 7:16PM MDT by NWS Denver CO","description":"SABCO\n\nTHE FOLLOWING MESSAGE IS TRANSMITTED AT THE REQUEST OF THE\nCOLORADO AVALANCHE INFORMATION CENTER.\n\n...A SPECIAL AVALANCHE ADVISORY IN EFFECT FROM 4:30 PM SATURDAY\nUNTIL 4:30 PM MONDAY...\n\n*WHAT...Avalanche conditions are dangerous above treeline.\n*WHERE...West Elk Mountains, Elk Mountains, San Juan Mountains, and\nLa Plata Mountains\n*WHEN...From 4:30 PM Saturday until 4:30 PM Monday.\n*IMPACTS...Backcountry travelers can easily trigger dangerous\navalanches large enough to bury a human.\n\n*PRECAUTION/PREPAREDNESS ACTIONS...A potent winter storm increases\navalanche danger Sunday into Monday. Rain, snow, and wind will cause\nnatural avalanches above treeline. Check the forecast at\nwww.colorado.gov/avalanche.\n\nBeyer & Pritchett","ends":"2026-11-02T16:30:00-07:00","severity":"Unknown","areaDesc":"West Elk an